 2.5Dataset and plant-level evaluation design
-Tasks. The primary analysis is a binary classification of infected (positive class) versus mock-inoculated plants, performed separately at 2, 4 and 6 dpi. We treat 2 and 4 dpi as presymptomatic because no sporulation was visible on either leaf surface at those times. Visible symptoms were not observed on the imaged adaxial surface at any time point, including 6 dpi; at 6 dpi, however, sporulation was visible on the abaxial surface, so 6 dpi is a transitional, later infection-stage reference and not a visually symptomatic imaging condition. We also evaluated a joint 2+4 dpi task, which combines the two presymptomatic time points and excludes 6 dpi, and, for one seed, a task with all dates pooled, which includes 6 dpi observations from a later infection stage (Table 3). Because the joint task uses information acquired at 4 dpi, it does not measure detection at 2 dpi alone. Within each time point, infected and mock-inoculated plants were imaged concurrently, so plant age and session are shared by both classes. The tasks share plants and are not independent of one another; the available paired observations differ at 2 dpi as specified in Table 3.
-Unit of analysis and splitting. The unit of analysis is the plant. Predicted infected-class probabilities were averaged across the available observations of each plant, including the available time points in the joint and pooled tasks, and thresholded at 0.5 before classification metrics were computed; AUROC used the unthresholded plant-mean probabilities. Missing observations were not imputed. All samples from a plant were confined to one partition. For each of three training seeds (157, 257 and 357), models were trained and evaluated on five plant-disjoint folds [CONFIRM: stratification by treatment; whether the fold assignment is fixed or re-drawn per seed and identical across tasks; whether any hyperparameters or checkpoints were selected on the held-out fold]. Out-of-fold predictions from the five folds were pooled to give one set of plant-level predictions per seed, from which all metrics were computed.
