 4Discussion
 4.1Principal findings
-Under plant-disjoint evaluation with concurrently imaged mock-inoculated controls, infected plants could be distinguished from mock-inoculated plants at 4 dpi, before visible symptoms, by every model that received RGB input (balanced accuracy 83.6–92.4%, AUROC 0.930–0.974), and at 6 dpi, a later infection stage with abaxial sporulation but a symptom-free imaged surface, by the same models (91.2–97.5%). At 2 dpi, none of these models did better than a balanced accuracy of 63.4%, and PSNet, the multimodal model evaluated here, was close to chance. These results support presymptomatic detection at 4 dpi in this dataset, but not at 2 dpi. They do not show that hyperspectral information was responsible for the 4 dpi result: RGB-only ResNets performed as well as the multimodal models, and the HSI-only models were at chance at 4 and 6 dpi. In this dataset the RGB stream carried most of the usable information.
+RGB-containing models distinguished the inoculated and mock groups at 4 dpi, before visible symptoms, and at 6 dpi, when sporulation was present on the lower surface but not on the imaged upper surface. PSNet was close to chance at 2 dpi. RGB-only models performed within the range of multimodal models, and the reported comparisons do not demonstrate an incremental benefit from HSI. The results establish within-run treatment-group discrimination, not confirmed infection-specific detection: tray, imaging order and unrecorded gain could also contribute.
 4.2The 2 dpi result and the reversed time course of the HSI-only models
-Two HSI-based models, the HSI Transformer and the compact 3D CNN, scored well at 2 dpi (AUROC 0.850 and 0.916) and then fell to near chance at 4 and 6 dpi. If an infection-related signal accumulated as the pathogen developed, the reverse would be expected. Several explanations are possible and cannot yet be separated. The result may reflect a transient early physiological response that has subsided by 4 dpi, although this would not explain the failure at 6 dpi, when infection was visible. It may instead reflect differences between the treatment groups that are specific to the 2 dpi acquisition, such as exposure or gain settings, illumination or warm-up state, plant position or imaging order. Without a white reference, per-sample exposure and gain logs, and a record of acquisition order (see section 2.3), such effects cannot be excluded. It may also reflect overfitting of high-capacity models to a small number of plants. Only the 2 dpi paired-data subset lacks observations present in the RGB inventory, as detailed in section 2.3. Until the within-dpi spectral analysis and model attribution are complete [UPDATE this paragraph with the outcome of the spectral statistics, acquisition-log checks and attribution analyses], we do not interpret the 2 dpi HSI-based scores as evidence of presymptomatic detection.
+The HSI Transformer and compact 3D CNN achieved AUROCs of 0.850 and 0.916 at 2 dpi and then performed much less well at later dates. Whole-cube spectral differences were also strongest at 2 dpi. A transient biological response is one possible explanation, but acquisition-specific variation, background, automatic gain and overfitting remain alternatives. Mock plants were always imaged first, so treatment and acquisition order were inseparable. Neither the spectral summaries nor the classification scores identify the cause. The incomplete 2 dpi paired-data coverage and unverified upstream preprocessing further limit interpretation. We therefore do not treat the 2 dpi HSI-only scores as evidence of an infection-specific early signal.
 4.3Role of the hyperspectral and RGB streams
-The dominance of RGB models is consistent with the practical difficulty of learning from a high-dimensional spectral cube in fewer than 100 plants, whereas pretrained ResNets bring spatial priors learned from natural images. It is also consistent with limitations of the hyperspectral data themselves: the cubes are uncalibrated intensities without a white reference, the band–wavelength mapping must be restored, and radiometric performance has not been characterised (Table 2). Because no visible symptoms were seen on the imaged upper surface at any time point, the RGB discrimination at 4 and 6 dpi cannot be attributed to visible lesions or pustules. It may reflect subtle differences in leaf colour, texture or posture caused by infection. It may equally reflect non-biological structure that a within-run split cannot exclude: infected and mock-inoculated plants were on separate trays, and the mock tray was always imaged first, so tray position, local growth conditions and any drift during a session all correlate with treatment. We therefore treat the 4 and 6 dpi RGB results as evidence that the two groups were separable in this dataset, and not as evidence of a spectral or biochemical signature. In this study the multimodal design offered no measurable benefit over RGB alone at 4 or 6 dpi, which contrasts with the commonly held expectation that fusion improves on single modalities. A definitive test of the value of HSI needs reflectance-calibrated acquisition and independent runs.
