 4.4Architectural components
-The ablation results do not support attributing performance to particular PSNet components. The full model did not exceed any variant at 2 dpi, 4 dpi or on the joint task, and the differences at 6 dpi were within seed variability and changed sign between variants. The plain multimodal model, which lacks the wavelet-initialised token and CAF, was as accurate as PSNet at 6 dpi and more accurate at 4 dpi. The results therefore do not support attributing gains to the wavelet-initialised token or to CAF. The 2D tokenisation variant changes token number, reduction stage and capacity together and cannot isolate the effect of 3D patching. The four extended ablations, when completed, will test single substitutions under a common protocol, but they use pooled dates and one seed and will therefore be informative about architectural sensitivity only, not about presymptomatic detection.
+The historical ablations do not establish a consistent benefit of individual PSNet components. Rankings change across tasks, and the alternative 2D path changes token count and parameterisation together. The controlled raw-spectrum token, current-layer CAF and fixed depth-averaging substitutions in the released code would address some of these ambiguities, but they have not produced completed results for this revision. The separate top-k experiment addresses input dimensionality rather than these architectural mechanisms. Its paired intervals include zero, its band sets vary by fold, and it uses only one training seed and pooled dates.
 4.5Effect of time-point pooling
 Pooling all dates raised PSNet’s accuracy from 71.88% (2+4 dpi) to 93.75% (all dates) for the same seed. The difference shows how strongly a headline accuracy depends on the inclusion of 6 dpi samples, and why the pooled figure cannot be presented as presymptomatic detection. The presymptomatic-only tasks are the relevant benchmark for this application. For these tasks, PSNet’s accuracy on the joint 2+4 dpi task (78.47%) was only modestly above the majority-class rate of 75%.
 4.6Implications for smart agriculture
-The imaging unit is inexpensive, repairable, compact and powered by USB-C, and its design files are open. These features make it attractive as a platform for controlled-environment phenotyping and for testing spectral sensing in greenhouse research, and the open, plant-level evaluation protocol used here can be reused for other sensors. The sub-£500 cost, of which the lens is about 78%, refers to the camera unit alone and excludes the laboratory-grade illumination source. Acquisition here required a dark room, fixed geometry, a 15 min warm-up and a 150 W halogen source (Table 13). The present work is therefore a low-cost imaging unit for controlled conditions and not a field-deployable system. Extension to greenhouse or field settings would require illumination that is either low-cost and stable or compensated for ambient light, reflectance standards in every session, logging of exposure and gain, tolerance to variable leaf orientation, and demonstration of performance across crops, genotypes, growth stages and confounding stresses [17,26,27]. Because the RGB stream alone provided most of the discrimination in this dataset, a practical consequence for system design is that inexpensive RGB sensing should be evaluated in parallel with hyperspectral sensing, but with the same caution about confounds.
